<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>aps-environment</key>
	<string>development</string>
	<key>com.apple.developer.aps-environment</key>
	<string>development</string>
	<key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-write</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.personal-information.addressbook</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- Write-only calendar access for an invite's "Add to Calendar", asked
	     for on that tap only. The app never reads the calendar. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.personal-information.calendars</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- ACCESS-GROUP RENAME, 2026-08-27. Read before changing this value again.

	     This group moved with the bundle id, from com.urlxl.mail. Keychain
	     items are scoped to their access group, and KeychainStorage /
	     GatedCredentialStore derive their service name from
	     Bundle.main.bundleIdentifier — so everything written by a pre-rename
	     build (device secret, gated credential, app-lock PIN verifier,
	     enrollment envelope, and the Secure Enclave key whose tag also moved
	     in EnrollmentVault) is now unreachable. Unreachable means it cannot be
	     read, and equally that unpair, "Reset KyPost on this device" and the
	     Hostile Location Protection erase cannot delete it: they delete by the
	     current service name only.

	     The decision, recorded so it is auditable rather than inferred: NO
	     migration or dual-group entitlement was added, because no build of
	     this app has ever reached an end user.

	     Two independent bases for that, neither of them an assumption. The
	     repository has no app release and no version tag (the only release is
	     the GopenPGP XCFramework), and no distribution workflow existed at all
	     until the one added in this same change. And the maintainer attested
	     on 2026-08-27 that no build has ever existed outside their control.
	     If that attestation is ever contradicted, so is this decision.

	     What that does NOT cover: developer machines that ran a pre-rename
	     build still hold those items, and the wipe paths will not reach them.
	     The residual exposure is bounded by the data-protection Keychain
	     itself — the items stay encrypted, this-device-only, and reachable
	     only by something signed with the old team-and-group pair, which no
	     longer exists — but the app's own "erase everything" promise does not
	     cover them, and on a machine that matters that gap should be closed by
	     hand.

	     If it ever turns out a build DID reach someone's device, this decision
	     is wrong and the other option is required: add
	     $(AppIdentifierPrefix)com.urlxl.mail as a second entry here, and a
	     one-time purge on launch and in the wipe paths that deletes every item
	     under the old service names and the old Secure Enclave key tag. -->
	<key>keychain-access-groups</key>
	<array>
		<string>$(AppIdentifierPrefix)org.kysecurity.mail</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
